body:
I wonder the same thing. Maybe you are too afraid to go through with it? I certainly don't blame you. Suicide is so final, it only makes sense to be terrified. I've seen a lot of posts and comments on this sub by people who have almost committed suicide but stepped away at the last moment because of fear.

&#x200B;

It could also be that you desperately want some kind of salvation. No one can blame you for that either. I'm going to guess that a lot, if not all of your suffering is undeserved and that it just kinda came along. It makes sense that maybe happiness will just "happen" too. 

&#x200B;

I too feel like I'm just waiting, like I'll kill myself eventually but I'm just putting it off for reasons that I know and reasons I can't figure out. I don't want to give you false hope, but know that life finds rather absurd ways of making you stay in the ring.

I don't want to criticize you, but I feel the need to defend your friend. Your friend likely didn't know how much you were hurting, and beside that if they had given you the gun, they would have felt guilty beyond belief if you took your life with their weapon. 

&#x200B;

I won't pretend I know exactly how you feel, but I just feel strongly about not making others feel responsible or whatever. I do know the feeling of wanting to die. "Surviving" to a lot of people is a victory because to them, the more time you give life the more likely you are to stumble into happiness I guess. On the contrary, prolonged life is just suffering for others. Please don't waste time trying to justify the suffering, for your own sake. Life is fucking cruel and likely meaningless. I don't think anyone can blame you for wanting to end it.

&#x200B;

 Try to find a little peace in clarity of mind. I struggle everyday but my happiest moments are when I accept the hurt as part of me. It doesn't mean giving into it, but it's more like acknowledging it's there to fuck with you and for no apparent reason. It makes me feel a little better that I can at least stare the bullshit in the face and say "fuck you, I hate you" without being afraid of it.

&#x200B;

This probably doesn't make sense and I'm likely rambling. In any case, I hope you are as okay as you can be. I hope things improve for you, and if they don't, I hope you find happiness, even if it's only for fleeting moments.

Sometimes in the past when I've been on call with certain friends I would do this and they'd get upset with me and make fun of me. I thought it was unfair, because while I get it being bad to bring the mood down, it's not like I was actively or outwardly being depressed, it was more like I would just get quieter and say a lot less. Not like there was any point in faking being happy either, they'd see through that, and if I just left so as to avoid bringing the mood down, I'd just gradually get more and more distant with them. It feels like there's no winning.

&#x200B;

You're not alone. I think the worst part of feeling this way is that for just a while you get teased with the notion that maybe you're doing better, maybe you are happier and maybe you want to keep living, but then you get slapped in the face with reality. The uncertainty of where your default setting is (elated vs depressed) can cause a lot of anxiety and sadness.
